A gap-based table works nicely here. `dp[left][right]` stores the best coins for the original array range from `left` through `right`. For each possible final balloon, the outside values are read directly, with `1` used when the range touches an edge.

The table is filled from one balloon to larger ranges. For `[3, 1, 5, 8]`, the single-balloon values are available before calculating ranges of length two and more.

```java
class Solution {
    public int maxCoins(int[] nums) {
        int n = nums.length;
        if (n == 0) {
            return 0;
        }

        int[][] dp = new int[n][n];

        for (int length = 1; length <= n; ++length) {
            for (int left = 0; left + length <= n; ++left) {
                int right = left + length - 1;
                int leftValue = left == 0 ? 1 : nums[left - 1];
                int rightValue = right == n - 1 ? 1 : nums[right + 1];

                for (int last = left; last <= right; ++last) {
                    int leftCoins = last == left ? 0 : dp[left][last - 1];
                    int rightCoins = last == right ? 0 : dp[last + 1][right];
                    int coins = leftCoins + rightCoins
                              + leftValue * nums[last] * rightValue;
                    dp[left][right] = Math.max(dp[left][right], coins);
                }
            }
        }

        return dp[0][n - 1];
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n^3)`. Space complexity: `O(n^2)`. This version leaves the input array unchanged and avoids adding padding values.

The main change in this version is the meaning of the state. The helper does not include its boundary balloons in the work. It only bursts balloons between them, which makes the base case very simple.

For `nums = [3, 1, 5, 8]`, the padded array is `[1, 3, 1, 5, 8, 1]`. If index `3` is the last balloon in the full interval, the final gain is `1 * 5 * 1`. The two recursive calls handle `[3, 1]` and `[8]` before that final burst.

```cpp
class Solution {
    vector<vector<int>> memo;
    vector<int> balloons;

    int solve(int left, int right) {
        if (left + 1 == right) {
            return 0;
        }

        if (memo[left][right] != -1) {
            return memo[left][right];
        }

        int best = 0;
        for (int last = left + 1; last < right; ++last) {
            int coins = balloons[left] * balloons[last] * balloons[right]
                      + solve(left, last)
                      + solve(last, right);
            best = max(best, coins);
        }

        memo[left][right] = best;
        return best;
    }

public:
    int maxCoins(vector<int>& nums) {
        int n = nums.size();
        balloons.assign(n + 2, 1);

        for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
            balloons[i + 1] = nums[i];
        }

        memo.assign(n + 2, vector<int>(n + 2, -1));
        return solve(0, n + 1);
    }
};
```

There are `O(n^2)` boundary pairs, and each one tries `O(n)` possible last positions, so the time cost is `O(n^3)`. The memo table uses `O(n^2)` space, with another `O(n)` for the call stack.

The empty array is covered too: the initial interval has no value between its two boundaries, so the result is `0`. The input vector is not modified.

I found it easier to reason about the last balloon rather than the first one. After all other balloons in `[left, right]` are gone, the last balloon gets multiplied by the fixed boundary values.

For example, with `[3, 1, 5, 8]`, if `5` is last, its final neighbors are the virtual `1`s. The left and right parts can be solved separately. Results are cached because the same ranges appear many times.

```java
class Solution {
    private int[][] memo;
    private int[] balloons;

    public int maxCoins(int[] nums) {
        int n = nums.length;
        balloons = new int[n + 2];
        balloons[0] = 1;
        balloons[n + 1] = 1;

        for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
            balloons[i + 1] = nums[i];
        }

        memo = new int[n + 2][n + 2];
        for (int i = 0; i < n + 2; ++i) {
            java.util.Arrays.fill(memo[i], -1);
        }

        return solve(1, n);
    }

    private int solve(int left, int right) {
        if (left > right) {
            return 0;
        }

        if (memo[left][right] != -1) {
            return memo[left][right];
        }

        int best = 0;
        for (int last = left; last <= right; ++last) {
            int coins = balloons[left - 1] * balloons[last] * balloons[right + 1]
                      + solve(left, last - 1)
                      + solve(last + 1, right);
            best = Math.max(best, coins);
        }

        memo[left][right] = best;
        return best;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n^3)`. Space complexity: `O(n^2)` for the memo table, plus `O(n)` recursion depth.

A useful edge case is an empty input array. It calls `solve(1, 0)` and correctly returns `0`.

This version keeps the two boundary positions in the state. `best(left, right)` means the maximum coins from balloons strictly between those positions.

The last balloon chosen inside the range gets `balloons[left] * balloons[last] * balloons[right]`. The cache avoids solving the same pair of boundaries again.

Dry run for `[3, 1]`: after padding, the range is `[1, 3, 1, 1]`. If the middle `1` is last, the final gain is `3 * 1 * 1`, plus the best result for the left and right smaller ranges.

```python
from functools import lru_cache

class Solution:
    def maxCoins(self, nums):
        balloons = [1] + nums + [1]

        @lru_cache(maxsize=None)
        def best(left, right):
            if left + 1 == right:
                return 0

            answer = 0
            for last in range(left + 1, right):
                coins = (balloons[left] * balloons[last] * balloons[right]
                         + best(left, last)
                         + best(last, right))
                answer = max(answer, coins)

            return answer

        return best(0, len(balloons) - 1)
```

There are `O(n^2)` pairs of boundaries, and each pair checks up to `n` possible last balloons. That gives `O(n^3)` time and `O(n^2)` space.

This code does not modify `nums`. It also handles `[0]` naturally, returning `0`.

The order of bursting changes the neighbors, so choosing the first balloon is awkward. I used the reverse view instead: choose the balloon that is burst last inside an interval. Its neighbors are then fixed by the two values outside the interval.

Two virtual balloons with value `1` handle the ends. The table stores the best result for every interval, and shorter intervals are ready before larger ones are calculated.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int maxCoins(vector<int>& nums) {
        int n = nums.size();
        vector<int> balloons(n + 2, 1);

        for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
            balloons[i + 1] = nums[i];
        }

        vector<vector<int>> dp(n + 2, vector<int>(n + 2, 0));

        for (int length = 1; length <= n; ++length) {
            for (int left = 1; left + length - 1 <= n; ++left) {
                int right = left + length - 1;

                for (int last = left; last <= right; ++last) {
                    int coins = balloons[left - 1] * balloons[last] * balloons[right + 1]
                              + dp[left][last - 1]
                              + dp[last + 1][right];
                    dp[left][right] = max(dp[left][right], coins);
                }
            }
        }

        return dp[1][n];
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n^3)` because every interval tries every possible last balloon. Space complexity: `O(n^2)`. The input vector is not changed.

